    <exercise number="191" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Two cars drive from one stoplight to the next, leaving at the same time and arriving at the same time. Is there ever a time when they are going the same speed? Prove or disprove.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Yes</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="192" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Show that $$y = \sec^{2}x$$ and $$y = \tan^{2}x$$ have the same derivative. What can you say about $$y = \sec^{2}x - \tan^{2}x?$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="193" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Show that $$y = \csc^{2}x$$ and $$y = \cot^{2}x$$ have the same derivative. What can you say about $$y = \csc^{2}x - \cot^{2}x?$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Let $$s1(t)s1(t)$$ represent the distance of Car 1 from Stoplight 1 at time $$tt$$ . Let $$s2(t)s2(t)$$ represent the distance of Car 2 from Stoplight 1 at time $$tt$$ . Then $$s1(0) = 0 = s2(0) s1(0) = 0 = s2(0)$$ . Let $$t=mt=m$$ represent the time that the cars arrive at Stoplight 2. Then $$s1(m) = d = s2(m) s1(m) = d = s2(m)$$ where $$dd$$ is the distance between the stoplights.</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Define a new function $$f(t) = s1(t) - s2(t) f(t) = s1(t) - s2(t)$$ . Then $$f(0)=0=f(m)f(0)=0=f(m)$$ . By the Mean Value Theorem, there is a time $$0&lt;c&lt;m0&lt;c&lt;m$$ such that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f′ (c) = f(m) - f(0) m-0 = 0m = 0. f′ (c) = f(m) - f(0) m-0 = 0m = 0.$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>At time c,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f′ (c) = 0 f′ (c) = 0$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$s1′ (c) - s2′ (c) = 0 s1′ (c) - s2′ (c) = 0$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$s1′ (c) = s2′ (c) s1′ (c) = s2′ (c)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>So, at time c, Car 1 and Car 2 are traveling at the same speed.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
